<Layout
  fullTitle="Xiaolongbao: Shanghai Soup Dumpling Guide"
  description="Everything about Shanghai xiaolongbao - the history, how to eat them, the best xiaolongbao restaurants, and what makes these soup dumplings so special."
>
  <main>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-20 md:pt-28 pb-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl tracking-tighter leading-none font-semibold text-slate-900">Xiaolongbao: Shanghai's Soup Dumpling Masterpiece</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mt-6 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">Xiaolongbao are the undisputed icons of Shanghai cuisine. Each delicate dumpling holds a secret: a spoonful of rich, savory broth that bursts forth with the first bite.</p>
        <AuthorByline />
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
      <div class="max-w-3xl prose prose-slate">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 mb-4">What Makes Xiaolongbao Special</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-500 leading-relaxed"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiaolongbao" rel="noreferrer">Xiaolongbao</a> (small steamer buns) are steamed dumplings traditionally filled with pork and a rich aspic that melts into hot broth during cooking. As <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiaolongbao" rel="noreferrer">Wikipedia</a> puts it, xiaolongbao are "a type of Chinese tangbao, traditionally prepared in a xiaolong, a small bamboo steaming basket." The wrapper is thin but strong enough to hold the soup without breaking. Each dumpling is traditionally folded with at least 18 pleats.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Eat Xiaolongbao</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-slate-500 leading-relaxed space-y-3">
          <li><strong>Pick it up gently</strong> with chopsticks at the topknot.</li>
          <li><strong>Dip it</strong> in black vinegar with ginger slivers.</li>
          <li><strong>Place it on your spoon</strong> and poke a small hole to release steam.</li>
          <li><strong>Sip the broth</strong> from the spoon enjoying the pure essence.</li>
          <li><strong>Eat the dumpling</strong> with more vinegar. Never bite it whole!</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Best Xiaolongbao in Shanghai</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">Jia Jia Tang Bao is the legend: crab roe xiaolongbao that locals queue an hour for. Din Tai Fung brings Taiwanese precision with exactly 18 folds per dumpling. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanxiang_Steamed_Bun_Restaurant" rel="noreferrer">Nanxiang Steamed Bun Restaurant</a> in the City God Temple is the historic original, serving since 1900.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Xiaolongbao Came From</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">Xiaolongbao were born in the 1870s in Nanxiang, a market town on Shanghai's western edge, where vendor Huang Mingxian thickened the traditional filling with aspic so that a spoonful of broth would form inside each bun as it steamed. The "Nanxiang steamed bun" became so famous that imitators set up stalls inside the Old City's City God Temple bazaar, and the dumpling spread from there across China and the world. The version eaten in Shanghai today is still closer to that original: a lighter, sweeter pork filling and a thinner, more delicate skin than the Cantonese dim-sum style found elsewhere.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">Variations Worth Ordering</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">Once you have tried the classic pork, work through the local favourites. <strong>Crab roe (zhuhuang)</strong> adds a rich, briny sweetness and is at its best during autumn hairy-crab season. <strong>Mushroom (xianggu)</strong> offers an earthy, lighter bite, while <strong>shrimp</strong> and the seasonal <strong>crayfish (xiaolongxia)</strong> fillings have become summer staples. Most shops serve six dumplings to a bamboo basket — a filling snack on its own, or the start of a larger spread alongside wonton soup and scallion pancakes.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Costs and When to Go</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">A basket of xiaolongbao runs roughly RMB 20–45 at a local shop and noticeably more at historic names like Nanxiang or the worldwide Din Tai Fung. Fillings, not the bun itself, drive the price: plain pork is cheapest, crab roe the priciest. Locals eat them at any hour, but the freshest, test-your-nerves queues form around the midday and early-evening peaks — go ten minutes before those rushes to beat the wait.</p>


